const T=(setup,assumptions,question,answer,reasoning)=>({setup:`Hypothetical exercise: ${setup}`,assumptions,question,answer,reasoning});
const L=(label,group,pages,definition,context,steps,formalism,question,answer,tasks)=>({label,group,pages,definition,context,steps,formalism,ruleCard:{question,answer},tasks});
export const curriculum=[
  ...firstRepairs.filter(r=>!(r.label in excluded)).map(r=>({...r,ruleCard:{question:ruleCards[r.label][0],answer:ruleCards[r.label][1]},group:r.label.includes('Balance Sheet')||r.label==='Actuarial Function (CRO)'?'functions':r.label==='Insurance value chain sequence'?'operations':'products'})),
  L('Role of insurance in the economy','foundations',[19,20],
    'Insurance pools defined risks and exchanges uncertain covered losses for a contractual promise financed by premiums; it also connects protection, savings and capital markets.',
    'One household may not be able to finance a large loss when it occurs. Insurance does not stop the loss: it changes who bears the covered financial cost under a contract. Combining many exposures helps estimate their aggregate cost. Premiums and investments then connect that protection mechanism to the company’s cash flows and the economy. Predictability of an average is different from certainty about one claim.',
    ['Specify the covered loss and the premium exchanged for the insurer’s promise.','Consider risks together rather than attempting to predict exactly which household will claim.','Estimate and fund obligations through pricing, reserving, assets and capital; the source also names savings, capital-market support and economic protection.'],
    'Source facts: pooling, the law of large numbers as a pricing tool, diversification, capital markets, saving/protection; the diagram identifies company functions. Foundational clarification: pooling does not eliminate the underlying physical loss or guarantee company solvency. The examples specify a simplified full-cover contract.',
    'Does risk transfer mean that the insured event no longer causes a loss?','No. The loss still occurs; the contract allocates its covered financial cost. The insurer must finance that promise and still faces uncertainty.',
    [T('A covered fire causes a CHF 20,000 loss.','A simplified contract reimburses the full covered loss; no deductible or limit.','What changes because of insurance?','The insurer pays CHF 20,000; the fire damage still exists.','The contract transfers the stated financial cost, not the physical event.'),T('The same fire occurs, but the contract excludes that loss.','The exclusion applies; no other policy covers it.','Can pooling make this insurer owe CHF 20,000 anyway?','No. The supplied contract does not cover this loss.','Pooling concerns aggregate uncertainty; it does not replace the contract’s coverage rule.')]),
  L('Insured-Insurer Relationship','foundations',[19,33],
    'In the slide’s simplified relationship, the insured pays a specified premium and the insurer undertakes uncertain claim payments under the product promise.',
    'The two arrows are different cash flows. A premium is the stated price for cover; a claim depends on whether a covered event occurs and what the contract pays. The slide labels the premium deterministic and claims stochastic within this simplified model. It does not say that the premium equals that customer’s eventual claim or that every real premium is fixed forever.',
    ['Read the premium arrow from insured to insurer.','Read the claim arrow in the opposite direction and identify its uncertainty.','Use the stated coverage trigger and amount to derive a claim payment; do not treat the premium as a personal deposit automatically returned.'],
    'Source convention: deterministic premium versus stochastic claims. Generalization limit: the diagram alone supplies no claim distribution, premium-changing rule or expected value. Exercises state their own event probability and payout rule.',
    'Why is a premium payment not a prediction of the customer’s actual claim?','The premium is the stated price of the promise; the actual claim depends on the covered event and payout rule. In the source model those payments are uncertain.',
    [T('Cover costs CHF 100; it pays CHF 5,000 on a specified event.','Event probability is 2%; otherwise payment is zero.','What is expected claim payment, and is actual payment necessarily CHF 100?','Expected payment is CHF 100; actual payment is CHF 0 or CHF 5,000.','0.02 × 5,000 = 100. An expected value is an average under the stated model, not the realized outcome.'),T('The same policy charges CHF 100 and states a CHF 5,000 benefit.','No event probability is supplied.','Can you calculate expected claim payment?','No. The event probability is missing.','A payout amount and price alone do not determine the probability-weighted expected claim.')]),
  L('Risk Pooling and Diversification','foundations',[19,20],
    'Pooling combines exposures; diversification concerns how different exposures move together. More contracts alone do not remove shared risk.',
    'A large pool can make average losses easier to estimate when risks do not all move together. But adding many policies exposed to the same flood can also add one shared loss scenario. The useful distinction is between the number of contracts and dependence between their losses. The slides connect pooling, diversification and the law of large numbers; they do not claim that all portfolios satisfy its conditions.',
    ['Combine exposures to reason about aggregate claims and their average.','Check whether a shared event can cause many claims together.','Distinguish improved average predictability from a guaranteed maximum loss, zero loss or sufficient capital.'],
    'Foundational clarification of the source’s law-of-large-numbers reference: an independent, identically distributed loss model with finite mean supports convergence of averages; the source does not establish those assumptions for a real insurer. The examples use expressly stated dependence, not an inferred portfolio model.',
    'Why does adding policies not necessarily diversify their losses?','The policies may share the same loss driver. Pool size counts exposures; diversification also requires examining how their losses occur together.',
    [T('100 buildings are all affected by the same specified flood.','In this scenario each building has a CHF 10,000 covered loss.','What is aggregate loss, and did the number of policies prevent it?','CHF 1,000,000; having 100 policies did not prevent the shared loss.','100 × 10,000 = 1,000,000. This common event exposes the difference between pooling and diversification.'),T('A proposed portfolio contains 100 buildings in several locations.','No dependence or shared-hazard information is provided.','Does the number 100 prove diversification?','No. Shared hazards and loss dependence still need assessment.','A policy count alone says nothing about whether losses move together.')]),
  L('Underwriting','operations',[20,33,36],
    'Underwriting decides whether to accept a risk and on what terms; pricing estimates the premium needed for the expected risk.',
    'A calculated price does not decide whether the company should write the policy. Underwriting applies risk-acceptance criteria and terms; pricing estimates the cost of the expected risk. Slide 20 groups assessment and pricing in one box, while the knowledge check separates their responsibilities. Keeping that distinction prevents treating a quoted premium as automatic acceptance.',
    ['Assess the submitted risk against acceptance criteria.','Decide acceptance, refusal or required terms under those criteria.','Coordinate with pricing for the premium; communicate the accepted terms to administration and claims.'],
    'The source does not provide universal underwriting thresholds or a legal right to refuse a policy. Every threshold below is an explicit hypothetical company rule.',
    'Does a computed premium automatically authorize risk acceptance?','No. Pricing estimates the premium; underwriting separately decides acceptance and terms under the supplied criteria.',
    [T('A risk has score 7 and a computed premium of CHF 400.','Company rule: accept only scores at most 5.','Does this rule permit acceptance at CHF 400?','No. Score 7 fails the acceptance criterion.','Computing a premium does not override the separately stated underwriting rule.'),T('The risk score changes to 4; the premium remains CHF 400.','Same rule: accept only scores at most 5; no other restriction.','How does the acceptance decision change?','The risk now satisfies the stated acceptance criterion.','4 is at most 5. The input affecting underwriting changed; the price did not.')]),
  L('Pricing','operations',[33,36],
    'Pricing estimates the premium needed for the expected risk; the source names expected claims, expenses and margin as components to cover.',
    'Accepting a risk and financing it are separate questions. Pricing translates the expected claim cost and other specified costs into a premium estimate. A price above expected claims alone can still be insufficient if it omits expenses. Conversely, covering expected costs in a model does not make actual losses certain or guarantee profit.',
    ['Estimate expected claims using the specified risk assumptions.','Include expenses and the stated margin basis.','Compare the premium with that modeled requirement, while keeping uncertainty and risk-acceptance decisions separate.'],
    'Slide 33 says premium must cover expected claims, expenses and margin; it does not give a universal formula or margin rate. The exercises use an additive currency margin explicitly, not a percentage of premium.',
    'Why is comparing a premium only with expected claims incomplete?','The source also includes expenses and margin. Their amounts and margin convention are needed before assessing the modeled premium requirement.',
    [T('Expected claims cost CHF 100 per policy; expenses CHF 20; margin CHF 10.','Premium equals these three currency amounts added together.','What is the modeled premium requirement?','CHF 130 per policy.','100 + 20 + 10 = 130. This is an expected-cost model, not guaranteed realized profit.'),T('Expected claims rise to CHF 120; expenses and margin are unchanged.','Same additive currency-margin rule.','What changes in the modeled premium?','It rises to CHF 150, an increase of CHF 20.','120 + 20 + 10 = 150. The changed claim assumption directly changes the modeled requirement.')]),
  L('Reserving','operations',[20,31,36],
    'Claims reserving estimates unpaid claim costs and recognizes the estimated obligation; it is distinct from funding and payment.',
    'A claim can exist before its final amount is known or paid. Reserving updates what the insurer is estimated to owe, using claims information and assumptions. The source diagram’s “set aside liabilities” wording refers to obligations, not a physical cash transfer. A correctly recorded provision does not tell us whether enough cash is available when settlement is due.',
    ['Estimate the remaining unpaid claim cost.','Revise the provision as the estimate changes, separating already paid amounts from what remains due.','Distinguish recognizing the liability, arranging its funding and settling it with cash.'],
    'Exercises assume a simplified claim provision equal to estimated ultimate claim cost minus payments already made, with no discounting, recoveries or other accounting adjustments. This is a declared teaching model, not a complete reserving standard.',
    'How does an unpaid-claim provision differ from available cash?','The provision estimates what remains owed. Cash is an asset used for payment. Recognizing a provision does not itself transfer cash or guarantee funding.',
    [T('A claim’s estimated total cost is CHF 12,000; CHF 4,000 has been paid.','Unpaid provision = estimated total cost minus payments; no other adjustment.','What unpaid cost should this model record?','CHF 8,000.','12,000 − 4,000 = 8,000. Previously paid costs are not still unpaid.'),T('Estimated total cost rises to CHF 15,000; payments remain CHF 4,000.','Same simplified rule; no new payment has occurred.','What changes in the provision and cash?','The provision rises to CHF 11,000; this estimate revision alone does not change cash.','15,000 − 4,000 = 11,000, up CHF 3,000. Revising the obligation differs from settling it.')]),
  L('Claims Management','operations',[20,31,33],
    'Claims management handles reported losses and payment under the applicable coverage; its information also informs reserves and experience analysis.',
    'When a loss is reported, the first useful question is what the policy actually promises for that event. Claims handling establishes relevant facts, applies the supplied terms and manages settlement. The resulting information helps estimate what remains unpaid. Paying claims, updating a provision and deciding whether to accept a new risk are different functions.',
    ['Identify the reported event and the applicable contract terms.','Determine the payment under those terms and manage settlement.','Feed payment and remaining-cost information into reserving and experience analysis.'],
    'The slides identify payment of losses and financial links to claims and reserving. The following deductible rule is explicitly hypothetical; actual eligibility, exclusions and settlement obligations require the contract and applicable rules.',
    'Why must claims handling examine contract terms rather than infer payment from the size of the loss?','The terms determine the covered event, exclusions, deductible and limit. A reported economic loss alone does not determine the insurer’s payment.',
    [T('A covered loss is CHF 3,000.','Payment = max(loss − CHF 500 deductible, 0); no limit.','What does the supplied rule pay?','CHF 2,500.','3,000 − 500 = 2,500. The loss and the insurer’s payment are different amounts.'),T('The covered loss is CHF 300.','Same CHF 500 deductible rule; no other benefit.','What changes in the payment?','Payment is CHF 0.','max(300 − 500, 0) = 0. A covered event does not necessarily produce a positive payment under this rule.')]),
  L('Investment Management','operations',[20,31,33],
    'Investment management invests available premiums and other assets while considering future claim payments, liquidity and asset risks.',
    'Premium cash may be received before claims are paid, creating an interval in which assets can be invested. The relevant question is not simply which investment has the highest return: cash-flow timing and market or credit risk affect whether those assets can support obligations. Investing a premium neither removes the claim liability nor guarantees a return.',
    ['Identify cash available for investment and expected payment dates.','Consider asset cash flows, liquidity, market risk and credit risk alongside return.','Reassess assets and obligations together through investment, ALM and reporting.'],
    'The source states objectives and functions, not an optimal asset allocation. Examples compare strictly supplied cash-flow schedules; they are not investment advice or a valuation of a real security.',
    'Why can a higher-return asset be unsuitable for an imminent claim payment?','Its cash flow or sale proceeds may arrive after the claim is due, or be uncertain. Return alone does not establish payment-date liquidity.',
    [T('CHF 1 million is due tomorrow; an asset repays CHF 1.1 million next year.','No sale, borrowing or other cash inflow is possible before tomorrow.','Can that future repayment alone fund tomorrow’s claim?','No. The receipt occurs after the payment date.','Future asset value does not supply cash by the stated deadline.'),T('Another asset pays CHF 1 million cash today.','Payment is received today; no other claims or expenses compete for it.','Does the timing conclusion change?','Yes. That cash is available before tomorrow’s CHF 1 million claim.','The changed payment date resolves the specified timing gap without proving anything about other obligations.')]),
  L('Property and Casualty Products','products',[23,25,35],
    'Property and casualty products address property loss and legal liability; the covered interest distinguishes own-property damage from injury or damage caused to others.',
    'Two losses in one accident can require different promises. Damage to the insured’s own car belongs to a property-damage question; damage the driver causes to another person is a liability question. The product-family table also highlights claim frequency and severity as distinct cost drivers. The family name alone does not settle what a specific policy pays.',
    ['Identify whose property or financial interest is affected.','Distinguish loss to the insured’s own property from covered liability to another party.','Specify the covered event and terms; analyze claim frequency separately from the cost per claim.'],
    'Slide 25 separates motor-vehicle damage and motor-vehicle liability. Product categories can overlap, and contract terms still govern payment. Illustrative expected claims cost below equals claim count times mean cost, with no other components.',
    'What distinguishes motor own-damage cover from motor liability in the source classification?','Own-damage concerns the insured vehicle’s loss; motor liability concerns covered injury or damage caused to others. They are separate product branches.',
    [T('A driver damages their own car and a neighbour’s fence.','Classify the losses using the source’s property-versus-liability distinction; do not decide payout.','Which loss belongs to each branch?','Own-car damage belongs to property/own-damage; liability for the neighbour’s fence belongs to liability.','The deciding feature is the affected interest, not simply that both losses arose from a car accident.'),T('A portfolio has 20 expected claims at CHF 1,000 mean cost. The mean cost becomes CHF 1,500.','Expected cost = count × mean cost; count stays 20.','Which cost driver changed and by how much does expected cost rise?','Severity changed; expected cost rises by CHF 10,000.','20 × 1,000 = 20,000; 20 × 1,500 = 30,000. Claim count did not change.')]),
  L('Health and Accident Insurance','products',[23,25],
    'Health and accident products address medical expense, disability or accident needs, including funding care or replacing income under the stated benefit.',
    '“Medical care” and “income replacement” answer different financial needs. One promise may reimburse eligible treatment costs, while another specifies a benefit for lost income. The table also links these products to claims experience and healthcare inflation. The label does not establish eligibility, compulsory cover or the size of a benefit.',
    ['Identify whether the stated need concerns medical costs, disability, accident or income replacement.','Read the benefit basis: expense reimbursement and a stated income benefit are not interchangeable.','Consider claims experience and cost inflation when interpreting the financial feature.'],
    'The source supplies family-level characteristics, not a jurisdiction’s insurance law. The examples state their own benefit clauses; none is inferred from the product label alone.',
    'Why does medical-expense reimbursement not automatically replace lost wages?','Those are different benefits. The supplied contract must include an income-replacement promise before that payment can be derived.',
    [T('A policy reimburses eligible medical expenses up to CHF 2,000. The insured loses CHF 1,000 wages.','The stated policy has no income-replacement benefit; no medical expense is claimed.','Does this clause reimburse the lost wages?','No. Lost wages are not the stated medical-expense benefit.','The financial need differs from the promise supplied in the contract.'),T('Eligible medical expense is CHF 1,500.','Same CHF 2,000 cap; no deductible or exclusion.','What does the stated clause reimburse?','CHF 1,500.','The expense fits the covered benefit and is below the supplied cap.')]),
  L('Reinsurance','products',[23,33,35],
    'Reinsurance protects an insurer against specified large, volatile or accumulated insurance losses through a separate risk-transfer promise.',
    'The customer of the protection is now an insurer. Reinsurance can change the amount of loss it retains, exchanging a ceded premium for specified claim recoveries. It is not a promise of profit or an automatic replacement of every obligation to policyholders. The retention and recovery depend on the supplied agreement; the slides give its purpose, not a universal contract structure.',
    ['Identify the insurer’s loss exposure and the portion it proposes to retain.','Apply the stated reinsurance recovery rule, considering its limit and scope.','Separate recovery, retained loss and ceded premium; reassess volatility and capital needs rather than assuming risk disappears.'],
    'The following layer is an explicitly hypothetical excess-of-loss rule: recovery = min(max(loss − retention, 0), limit). It is supplied to solve the exercises, not attributed to the introductory slides.',
    'Why does reinsurance not imply that an insurer retains no loss?','Retention, scope and limits can leave loss with the insurer; ceded premium is also a cost. The actual recovery requires the agreement’s rule.',
    [T('A loss is CHF 8 million; retention is CHF 3 million and cover limit CHF 4 million.','Recovery = min(max(loss − retention, 0), limit); ignore premium and other costs.','What are recovery and retained loss?','Recovery is CHF 4 million; retained loss is CHF 4 million.','The layer would otherwise cover 8 − 3 = 5 million, but its 4 million limit binds; 8 − 4 = 4 million remains.'),T('The loss falls to CHF 2 million under the same layer.','Same CHF 3 million retention and CHF 4 million limit.','What changes in recovery?','Recovery is zero; the insurer retains CHF 2 million.','The loss is below retention, so the supplied layer is not reached.')]),
  L('Captive Insurance','products',[23,27],
    'Captive insurance finances a corporate group’s own risks through an insurance arrangement within that group, with external protection when needed.',
    'Putting the risk in a group-owned insurer can change how it is financed and managed, but a payment from one group entity to another does not by itself remove the group’s economic loss. The table distinguishes captive retention from external protection. This is a different question from whether a separate reinsurer bears part of a loss.',
    ['Identify the corporate group whose risks are being financed.','Distinguish the captive’s accounting position from the group’s consolidated economic exposure.','Identify any external risk transfer separately; an internal transfer alone is not external protection.'],
    'The source defines the family’s need and retained-risk feature; it does not prescribe the legal form or tax treatment of every captive. Exercises state a simplified consolidated-group perspective and ignore expenses.',
    'Does moving a loss into a wholly owned captive eliminate it for the corporate group?','No. An internal allocation does not itself transfer the loss outside the group. External protection must be identified separately.',
    [T('A parent has a CHF 1 million loss and receives CHF 1 million from its wholly owned captive.','No external recovery; examine the consolidated group, ignoring expenses.','Has the group’s CHF 1 million economic loss disappeared?','No. The payment reallocates resources inside the group.','The captive and parent belong to the same consolidated group; no outside party bears the loss.'),T('An external reinsurer reimburses CHF 0.6 million for the same group loss.','Reimbursement is received; ignore premiums and expenses.','How much loss remains with the group?','CHF 0.4 million.','1 − 0.6 = 0.4 million. Unlike the internal payment, this recovery crosses the group boundary.')]),
  L('Insurance Product Types','products',[23,25,35],
    'Insurance products can be classified by the covered need and interest; the source diagram separates Life, Health/Accident, Property and Liability while allowing overlapping groupings.',
    'A useful classification identifies what the contract protects, not just the word “insurance”. Slide 25 separates motor-vehicle damage from motor-vehicle liability and individual from group Life. The table on slide 23 adds customer need, risk or benefit, and financial feature. Its warning that classifications overlap prevents treating one diagram as a universal legal taxonomy.',
    ['Identify the covered person or interest and the event or benefit.','Use the appropriate branch: Life, medical/accident need, own-property damage or liability to others.','Keep examples inside their family and check actual terms before deriving a payment.'],
    'Visible examples: individual/group Life; Accident/Health; property Fire, Theft, Water damage, Glass breakage, Motor vehicle damage, Valuables/art, Transportation, Technical/industry, Operating losses; liability Private, Corporate, Professional, Motor vehicle, Building, Directors/officers, Legal protection, Credit/guarantees. These are source categories, not a payout determination.',
    'Why does “motor” alone not determine the product branch?','The diagram includes both damage to the vehicle and liability arising from its use. The protected interest distinguishes those branches.',
    [T('A proposed policy covers damage to the policyholder’s own motor vehicle.','Classify only using the diagram’s stated branches.','Is this the motor-liability branch?','No. It is the motor-vehicle-damage branch under property.','The policy addresses the insured’s own vehicle rather than liability to others.'),T('The proposed promise instead covers injury caused to another person by the driver.','Classify the stated interest; do not assume that all exclusions are known.','Which branch now applies?','Motor-vehicle liability.','The protected exposure is liability to someone else, although the event still involves a car.')]),
  L('Term life insurance','products',[23,35],
    'Term life pays the stated death benefit if the insured dies during the fixed coverage period, subject to the supplied contract conditions.',
    'The decisive condition is both the event and its timing. “Life insurance” is the broad family; term life is the example where death during a fixed period triggers the benefit. The knowledge check does not say that surviving to expiry earns the death benefit, or that all premiums and contract terms are universally fixed.',
    ['Identify the start and end of the stated coverage period.','Check whether death occurs within that period and any supplied eligibility conditions.','Distinguish a death benefit from a savings or survival benefit not included in the stated promise.'],
    'The source does not supply universal exclusions, premium patterns, legal entitlements or a maximum term. Exercises are simplified clauses whose only benefit is expressly stated.',
    'What two conditions distinguish the simplified term-life payout rule?','The covered event is the insured’s death, and it must occur during the fixed coverage period. A savings payment at expiry is a different promise.',
    [T('Cover pays CHF 80,000 on death during years 1–10. Death occurs in year 8.','Cover is in force; no exclusion applies.','Does the stated benefit trigger?','Yes: CHF 80,000.','Death is the covered event and year 8 lies within the stated coverage period.'),T('Death instead occurs in year 12.','Same years 1–10 cover; no extension or other benefit.','Does this term cover trigger?','No. The event occurs after expiry.','Changing the event date crosses the coverage boundary; the benefit amount alone does not establish entitlement.')]),
  L('Motor liability insurance','products',[25,35],
    'Motor liability concerns covered injury or damage caused to others by an insured driver; own-vehicle damage is a separate product branch.',
    'After an accident, the same driver may have damage to their own car and liability for harm to another person. The source’s motor-liability question concerns the second exposure. Asking who was harmed and what interest is covered avoids assuming that this policy repairs the driver’s own vehicle.',
    ['Identify the injured party or damaged property.','Distinguish the driver’s own loss from covered liability to others.','Apply the stated liability promise and its terms before calculating payment.'],
    'The supplied source does not determine compulsory-cover rules, fault standards or a real claim’s eligibility. Exercises classify expressly stated covered interests, not legal liability in a real case.',
    'Does motor liability by itself establish cover for the insured’s own damaged car?','No. The source distinguishes liability to others from own-vehicle damage. Own-damage cover would require a separate stated promise.',
    [T('A policy covers liability for damage caused to others. The driver’s own car has CHF 2,000 damage.','No own-damage benefit is included.','Does this stated promise pay for the own-car damage?','No. It is outside the stated covered interest.','The driver’s own vehicle is not a third party’s loss.'),T('Instead, covered damage to another person’s fence is CHF 2,000.','Liability and coverage are stipulated; the clause pays the full covered amount, with no deductible or limit.','What does the stated clause pay?','CHF 2,000.','The changed affected interest now fits the supplied liability promise.')]),
  L('Customer need and product','products',[23,33],
    'Product design specifies a promise addressing a protection or savings need; its terms define future cash flows and involve product, distribution, legal and compliance functions.',
    'A product cannot be priced coherently before its promise is clear. Does the customer need money on death, reimbursement of a property loss, medical funding or a savings benefit? The product stage specifies that promise. Different triggers, limits or durations change later claim cash flows and therefore the information pricing, underwriting, reserves and investment need.',
    ['Identify the customer’s protection or savings need.','Specify the covered trigger, benefit and duration with the relevant functions.','Pass the defined cash-flow promise into pricing and downstream operations; do not treat product design as actuarial estimation alone.'],
    'Slide 33 names product, distribution, legal and compliance and says product terms define future cash flows. It does not supply a universally suitable customer product or specific advice. Exercises compare stated promises only.',
    'Why must product terms be known before expected claim cash flows can be estimated?','The terms define what event triggers payment, its amount and duration. Changing that promise can change the cash flows whose cost must be estimated.',
    [T('Promise A pays CHF 10,000 on event X; promise B pays CHF 20,000 on the same event.','Event probability is 5%; no other benefits or costs.','How do the expected payments differ?','A expects CHF 500; B expects CHF 1,000.','0.05 × 10,000 = 500; 0.05 × 20,000 = 1,000. Changing the product amount changes the cash-flow promise.'),T('Both promises now pay CHF 10,000, but their triggers differ.','No probabilities for those different triggers are supplied.','Can equal benefit amounts establish equal expected costs?','No. The event probabilities and other terms are needed.','Equal amounts do not make different payment conditions equivalent.')]),
  L('Top European Insurers by Premium Income (2022)','markets',[26],
    'The source chart ranks the displayed European insurers by 2022 premium income, measured in USD billions, rather than by profit or service quality.',
    'Read the metric before reading the ranking. AXA’s displayed 106.2 and Allianz’s 100.6 are premium-income amounts in USD billions for 2022. The orange bars identify Swiss insurers; they do not represent a better financial result. A ranking of the shown insurers does not provide the denominator for the entire European market or a current-year ranking.',
    ['Identify the chart year, premium-income metric and USD-billion unit.','Compare amounts within that same basis; distinguish a currency difference from a percentage share.','Separate shown premium volume from missing information about profit, claims, solvency, service or the full market.'],
    'The original page-26 image supplies the displayed values and legend. AXA 106.2, Allianz 100.6, Generali 85.2; displayed Swiss insurers include Zurich 58.9, Chubb 52.0 and Swiss Re 47.9. These are source-year observations, not current market facts.',
    'What does having the largest bar in this chart actually establish?','The largest displayed 2022 premium income on the chart’s USD-billion basis. It does not establish the highest profit or a share of the whole market.',
    [T('Use the source-year chart values: AXA 106.2 and Allianz 100.6.','Both are premium income in USD billions for 2022.','What is their displayed difference?','USD 5.6 billion.','106.2 − 100.6 = 5.6; this is an amount difference, not 5.6 percentage points.'),T('An analyst wants AXA’s share of the whole European market from its 106.2 bar.','The whole-market premium total is not provided.','Can the market share be calculated from the largest bar alone?','No. The whole-market denominator is missing.','A ranking or selected-company chart does not supply the total needed for a market-share ratio.')]),
  L('Types of Insurance Companies in Switzerland','markets',[23,27],
    'The course distinguishes Life, property/casualty, Health, Reinsurance and Captives by the protection need and risk-bearing role.',
    '“Insurance company” is not a single customer model. A Life insurer addresses the source’s income or savings needs; property/casualty addresses property and liability; Health concerns medical needs; a reinsurer protects an insurer; a captive finances its corporate group’s own risks. The company-count table is a statistical classification with its own scope, not a map of every product a firm may offer.',
    ['Identify whose need is being protected: individual, other insurer or corporate group.','Distinguish the covered risk or benefit rather than relying only on a business label.','Keep the lecture’s product-family overview separate from the FINMA-subject count categories and their date.'],
    'The page-27 table counts companies subject to FINMA as of December 31; its Health row is specifically supplementary health. Do not silently treat that row as all healthcare insurers or add captive counts to the total again.',
    'What distinguishes a reinsurer’s protection need from a captive’s in the source?','Reinsurance protects an insurer against specified insurance losses; captive insurance finances a corporate group’s own retained risks. The customer and group boundary differ.',
    [T('Firm A’s stated purpose is protecting another insurer against large insurance losses.','Use the course family descriptions.','Which family fits that stated need?','Reinsurance.','The protected customer is an insurer, rather than a household or the firm’s own corporate group.'),T('Firm B instead finances only its parent group’s own risks.','That within-group purpose is stipulated; classify the need only.','Which family fits now?','Captive insurance.','The group-boundary input changes the classification even though both arrangements concern risk financing.')]),
  L('Insurance Companies Statistics 2023 vs 2024','markets',[27],
    'The source table counts FINMA-subject insurance companies at December 31 in 2023 and 2024; changes in counts do not explain their cause.',
    'A count of companies is different from a share of premiums. The table reports 195 in 2024 versus 194 in 2023. Its five category rows explain that total, while the 143 domiciled companies and 52 foreign branches in 2024 are an “of which” breakdown. Adding those subsets to 195 would count the same population again.',
    ['Fix the population and dates before comparing the columns.','Compare each category: Life 18/18, Non-life 100/99, supplementary Health 27/29, Reinsurance 23/24, Captives 27/24.','Reconcile the total and distinguish its subsets; do not infer company exits, failures or competition effects without additional evidence.'],
    '2024 total: 18 + 100 + 27 + 23 + 27 = 195. 2023 total: 18 + 99 + 29 + 24 + 24 = 194. Domiciled 143/143 and foreign branches 52/51 are subsets. A statistical difference does not identify its causal explanation.',
    'Why must the “of which” rows not be added to the total number of companies?','They partition the already counted population. In 2024, 143 domiciled companies plus 52 foreign branches already equals the total 195.',
    [T('The table reports 195 total companies, of which 143 domiciled and 52 foreign branches.','All refer to the same 2024 FINMA-subject population.','Is the population 390 after adding all three numbers?','No. It is 195.','143 + 52 = 195; adding the total to its own breakdown duplicates the population.'),T('Supplementary-health counts are 29 in 2023 and 27 in 2024.','Only those counts are supplied.','What change is established, and are two insolvencies established?','A decrease of 2 companies; insolvencies are not established.','27 − 29 = −2. Counts alone do not say whether changes reflect exits, mergers, classification or another cause.')]),
  L('Market shares for non-life insurers','markets',[28,35],
    'The shown non-life shares measure gross premiums written in 2025, excluding supplementary health and accident insurance.',
    'This chart compares premium volume within a specified segment, not all insurance activity. Die Mobiliar is 19.3%, AXA 18.3% and Zurich 13.1% on the displayed 2025 basis. The exclusion of supplementary health and accident insurance matters: changing the denominator can change a share even if the insurer’s own premium amount is unchanged.',
    ['Identify gross premiums written, 2025 and the explicit segment exclusions.','Read the individual company slices separately from the combined “Others” slice.','Compare premium shares without converting them into profit, satisfaction or solvency rankings.'],
    'Source image: Die Mobiliar 19.3%, AXA 18.3%, Zurich 13.1%, Helvetia 10.6%, Allianz 9.9%, Baloise 6.8%, Vaudoise 4.1%, Others 17.7%. These are the chart’s historical observations. The exercises use the stated percentages or an explicitly changed denominator.',
    'Why is the chart’s exclusion of supplementary health and accident insurance part of the definition of the share?','It defines which premiums enter the market denominator. Adding excluded business would change the comparison basis and may change the calculated share.',
    [T('Use the source shares: Die Mobiliar 19.3%, AXA 18.3%, Zurich 13.1%.','Same shown segment and year.','What share do these three companies jointly represent?','50.7%.','19.3 + 18.3 + 13.1 = 50.7. “Others” is a combined slice, not an additional individual company.'),T('A firm has 20 premium units in a 100-unit segment; the defined market expands to 125 units.','The firm’s 20 units stay fixed; use premium / market premium.','What happens to its measured share?','It falls from 20% to 16%.','20 / 125 × 100 = 16. The denominator changed; the firm’s own volume did not.')]),
  L('Top 3 players in Swiss insurance market','markets',[28,29,30],
    'A top-three ranking requires a specified insurance segment, year and metric; the course supplies separate 2025 premium-share charts.',
    'There is no one top-three answer for all insurance business. In the shown non-life segment the leaders are Die Mobiliar, AXA and Zurich; in Life they are Swiss Life, Helvetia and AXA; in Reinsurance they are Swiss Re Group, NewRe and MS Reinsurance. The classroom prompt also mentions Health and Accident, but these pages do not provide separate charts for those rankings.',
    ['Choose the segment and the common gross-written-premium/year basis.','Rank individual companies rather than treating “Others” as one firm.','State when a requested segment lacks data instead of borrowing the non-life ranking or searching a different year silently.'],
    'Scope: 2025 charts on pages 28–30, with page 28 excluding supplementary health and accident insurance. Do not present these rankings as current universal market leaders or infer separate Health/Accident leaders from excluded data.',
    'What information is needed before answering “Who are the top three insurers?”','The segment, year, metric and coverage of the dataset. Without those, Life, non-life and Reinsurance can give different correct rankings.',
    [T('Use the three displayed 2025 charts.','Rank individual companies by their displayed premium shares.','Does AXA lead all three markets?','No. The shown leaders are Die Mobiliar (non-life), Swiss Life (Life) and Swiss Re Group (Reinsurance).','A large position in one segment does not establish leadership in another.'),T('A question asks for separate accident-insurer leaders.','Only the supplied pages 28–30 may be used.','Can that ranking be answered from these charts?','No. A separate relevant dataset is missing.','The non-life chart explicitly excludes accident insurance; the other charts concern Life and Reinsurance.')]),
  L('Market Shares for Life Insurers','markets',[29,35],
    'The Life chart reports each insurer’s 2025 share of gross premiums written; a high share does not establish profit, service quality or solvency.',
    'Swiss Life’s 41.8% is a share of the displayed Life premium market, not a percentage profit margin or share of all insurance types. The source knowledge check expressly rejects inferring greatest profitability, best service or strongest solvency from the largest Life share. Read volume, performance and financial strength as different questions.',
    ['Identify the Life segment, gross-written-premium metric and 2025 year.','Read the shares: Swiss Life 41.8%, Helvetia 11.8%, AXA 10.0% are the three largest shown companies.','Distinguish a percentage-point difference from a relative percentage change; require separate evidence for performance claims.'],
    'Remaining source shares: Baloise 8.2%, Allianz Suisse 7.3%, Zurich 5.8%, Die Mobiliar 4.4%, Generali 4.3%, Pax 4.3%, Others 2.1%. The chart and review supply no profit, satisfaction or solvency measurements.',
    'Does the largest shown Life premium share establish the strongest solvency?','No. It establishes the largest shown premium share on that basis. A solvency assessment requires different financial and risk information.',
    [T('Swiss Life is 41.8% and Helvetia 11.8% on the source chart.','Same Life market and 2025 basis.','What is their share difference in percentage points?','30.0 percentage points.','41.8 − 11.8 = 30.0. This is not a statement that profit differs by 30%.'),T('An insurer’s premium share rises from 10% to 12%.','Compare the same market definition and period basis.','How do the point increase and relative increase differ?','2 percentage points; a 20% relative increase.','12 − 10 = 2 points; (12 − 10) / 10 × 100 = 20%. The denominators answer different questions.')]),
  L('Market Shares for Reinsurers in Switzerland','markets',[30,35],
    'The source chart reports reinsurers’ 2025 gross-written-premium shares in Switzerland; Swiss Re Group’s displayed share is 63.4%.',
    'A 63.4% premium share describes a large position in the displayed reinsurance segment. It does not mean that this company pays 63.4% of all Swiss household claims or holds 63.4% of every insurer’s capital. Reinsurance is a distinct customer and risk-transfer market, and the chart measures premiums rather than claim payments.',
    ['Fix the Reinsurance segment, gross-written-premium metric and 2025 year.','Read the largest shown companies: Swiss Re Group 63.4%, NewRe 12.1%, MS Reinsurance 5.5%.','Keep market-volume concentration distinct from loss allocation, profit or solvency.'],
    'Further source shares: RenaissanceRe 4.6%, UNIQA 3.6%, Euler Hermes 3.5%, Others 7.3%. The chart does not supply ceded-loss contract shares or a current-year ranking.',
    'Why does a 63.4% reinsurance premium share not determine the company’s share of claim payments?','Premium volume and claim payments are different metrics. Claim allocations depend on losses and contract terms, which the market-share chart does not provide.',
    [T('Use Swiss Re Group 63.4%, NewRe 12.1% and MS Reinsurance 5.5%.','Same displayed 2025 premium market.','What is the three-company combined share?','81.0%.','63.4 + 12.1 + 5.5 = 81.0. This measures premium volume on the chart’s basis.'),T('A reader applies Swiss Re Group’s 63.4% directly to a CHF 1 million loss.','Only the market-share chart is supplied; no reinsurance contract is given.','Is a CHF 634,000 recovery established?','No. Recovery cannot be derived from market share.','A company’s market denominator is not the allocation rule of a particular reinsurance agreement.')]),
  L('Premium market share','markets',[28,29,30,35],
    'Premium market share is an insurer’s written premiums divided by the written premiums of the specified market on a consistent basis.',
    'A share is a ratio with a numerator and denominator. The insurer can write more premiums while losing share if the defined market grows faster. The course’s charts specify gross premiums written, segment and year, so those qualifiers belong to the concept rather than being optional labels. This ratio measures volume, not claims paid or customer satisfaction.',
    ['Select the market’s segment, period and gross/net premium basis.','Use the insurer’s comparable premiums as numerator and the whole defined market as denominator.','Explain changes in both quantities; distinguish volume, share and percentage-point changes.'],
    'Share (%) = comparable insurer written premiums / total written premiums in the specified market × 100. The exercises supply the totals. Combining unlike years, segments or premium bases does not calculate the stated share.',
    'Can an insurer’s premiums grow while its market share falls?','Yes. If the comparable market denominator grows faster, the insurer’s ratio falls despite a higher premium amount.',
    [T('An insurer writes 10 units in a 100-unit market, then 12 in a 150-unit market.','Both periods use the same segment and gross-written-premium basis.','How does its share change despite premium growth?','From 10% to 8%, a decrease of 2 percentage points.','10 / 100 × 100 = 10%; 12 / 150 × 100 = 8%. The market grew faster than the insurer.'),T('The insurer’s 12 units are gross premiums; the market total is 150 net-premium units.','No conversion between the bases is supplied.','Is 12/150 a valid gross-premium market share?','No. The numerator and denominator use different bases.','Comparable measurement is required; a computable ratio can still answer the wrong question.')]),
  L('Market Overview','challenges',[19,21],
    'The course’s market overview names macroeconomic developments, demographics, technology and sustainability as external drivers of insurance needs and risks.',
    'These are inputs to the insurance business rather than departments inside it. A changing cost environment can affect claims assumptions; a technology change can affect processes or create new exposures. The overview names driver categories but does not predict how demand or profit must change. The challenges slide provides narrower links that can actually be traced.',
    ['Identify whether the change is an external driver or an internal company response.','Trace the affected promise, cost assumption, asset cash flow or process when a link is given.','State what remains unknown instead of concluding that every external change has the same effect.'],
    'Source links on page 21: low/negative rates affect investment return, especially guaranteed Life products; inflation affects claims costs and asset-liability mismatches. Demographics, technology and sustainability are overview categories, not source-derived numeric forecasts.',
    'Why is “the market changed” insufficient to derive an insurer’s financial result?','The affected driver, exposure, contract and management assumptions are needed. A category label does not determine which cash flow changes or by how much.',
    [T('Claim-repair costs rise while a firm replaces its internal claims software.','Classify inflation as an external cost driver and software replacement as an internal response.','Are these the same kind of change?','No. One changes the cost environment; the other changes a company process.','Separating driver and response prevents treating a department action as an external market condition.'),T('A slide names changing demographics but gives no portfolio data.','No effect size or relevant product exposure is specified.','Can next year’s premium revenue be calculated?','No. The category does not supply a numeric prediction.','Exposure, customer behavior and product assumptions would be needed to connect the driver to revenue.')]),
  L('Macroeconomic & finance market challenges','challenges',[19,21],
    'Macroeconomic and financial conditions can change investment returns and the cost or timing of insurance obligations; the source highlights low/negative interest rates and inflation.',
    'An insurer can promise future benefits under one set of economic conditions and invest or pay claims under another. The source’s low-rate example concerns investment-return pressure, particularly for Life products with guarantees; its inflation example concerns claims costs and asset-liability mismatches. Neither statement supplies a universal prediction for every bond price or the insurer’s total profit.',
    ['Identify the affected financial channel rather than calling all changes “market risk”.','Compare expected asset cash flows with the relevant obligation or guarantee.','Keep investment-return assumptions, market-value changes and claims-cost changes distinct.'],
    'Exercises specify one-period simple cash returns, with no bond valuation, tax, fees or other assets. The source does not establish the direction of every existing asset’s market value after an interest-rate change.',
    'Why can lower reinvestment returns matter for a guaranteed future benefit?','The return assumed to support the promised payment can fall while the stated promise remains unchanged, creating a funding gap under the supplied cash-flow model.',
    [T('CHF 1 million must fund CHF 1.02 million in one year. Available simple return is 1%.','One-period cash model; no other inflow or expense.','What gap remains at the payment date?','CHF 10,000.','1,000,000 × 1.01 = 1,010,000 versus 1,020,000 due.'),T('The available return becomes 3% under the same model.','Same principal and payment; no other changes.','How does the funding comparison change?','Assets reach CHF 1.03 million, CHF 10,000 above the stated payment.','1,000,000 × 1.03 = 1,030,000. This supplied cash-flow comparison is not a guarantee about a real portfolio.')]),
  L('Inflation rising','challenges',[21,31],
    'Inflation can raise claim costs and contribute to asset-liability mismatches; its financial effect depends on the claims and assets affected.',
    'A repair claim priced under an old cost level may cost more when it is settled. The source explicitly connects inflation to claim costs and asset-liability mismatches. The useful mechanism is the changed cost assumption and the assets available to support it, rather than an automatic rule that every premium rises or every insurer becomes insolvent.',
    ['Identify which claim-cost component is assumed to rise and by how much.','Revise the estimated obligation under the stated model.','Compare with supporting asset cash flows; distinguish the estimate revision from a cash payment today.'],
    'A stated inflation assumption is not proof that all claim types rise at the same rate. Examples use a uniform proportional change solely for the given repair estimate, with no tax, discounting or investment changes.',
    'Does revising a claim estimate for inflation itself pay the claim or supply extra assets?','No. It changes the estimated cost or liability under the model. Payment and asset funding remain separate questions.',
    [T('An unpaid repair estimate is CHF 10,000; its relevant cost level rises 10%.','The estimate scales proportionally; no payments or other changes.','What is the revised estimate?','CHF 11,000, up CHF 1,000.','10,000 × 1.10 = 11,000. This revision alone does not transfer cash.'),T('The claim instead has a stipulated fixed CHF 10,000 benefit unaffected by repair prices.','The fixed contractual amount is the only benefit; no change to terms.','Does the same 10% repair-cost assumption raise this benefit?','No. The supplied benefit remains CHF 10,000.','The changed condition is the payment basis: a fixed benefit is not the same as a repair-cost-linked estimate.')]),
  L('Volatility & uncertainty','challenges',[21,34],
    'Volatility and uncertainty concern variation and incomplete knowledge of outcomes, not merely the size of their average.',
    'Two portfolios can have the same expected cost but very different adverse outcomes. The challenges slide names Covid, geopolitical tensions and the energy crisis as uncertainty contexts; the actuarial slide calls for explaining uncertainty and trade-offs. A point estimate alone does not identify the possible range or the resources needed in a worse scenario.',
    ['Separate the expected value from the set of possible outcomes.','Compare the stated probabilities and downside scenarios.','Explain what remains uncertain and test decisions across supplied scenarios rather than treating the mean as a maximum.'],
    'The course does not supply a universal probability distribution for these events. The exercises define their own two-outcome models; “volatility” here is a comparison of variation, not a regulatory capital computation.',
    'Can two exposures have the same expected loss but different variation?','Yes. Expected value averages probability-weighted outcomes; the range and probability of adverse outcomes can differ even when the average is equal.',
    [T('A costs CHF 100 with certainty. B costs CHF 0 or CHF 200, equally likely.','Use only these stipulated outcomes.','Do equal expected costs imply equal variation?','No. Both expect CHF 100, but B varies and A does not.','B’s mean is 0.5 × 0 + 0.5 × 200 = 100; its outcome is not certain.'),T('B’s CHF 200 outcome probability rises from 50% to 75%.','Its other outcome remains CHF 0; no additional outcomes.','What happens to its expected cost?','It rises to CHF 150.','0.75 × 200 + 0.25 × 0 = 150. A changed probability alters the mean as well as the outcome uncertainty.')]),
  L('Regulatory and Capital requirements','challenges',[21,31],
    'Regulatory and capital requirements create constraints that must be assessed using the applicable rules and capital basis; the source supplies no universal threshold.',
    'Knowing that capital is positive is different from knowing that it meets a requirement. The challenges slide names regulatory and capital requirements, and the functions slide links capital management to solvency. A useful assessment states both available capital and the required amount on the same basis. It cannot substitute profit or asset value for that comparison.',
    ['Identify the applicable requirement and the capital measure it uses.','Compare available and required capital on a consistent basis.','Distinguish a model shortfall from a complete legal or solvency conclusion about a real insurer.'],
    'All numeric thresholds below are hypothetical, not a statement of Swiss or other law. The course does not supply an actual capital formula, current requirement or enforcement consequence.',
    'Why does positive capital alone not establish compliance with a capital requirement?','The required amount and measurement basis may be higher or different. Both the available amount and the applicable requirement are needed.',
    [T('A model reports available capital CHF 6 million and required capital CHF 8 million.','Both amounts use the same stipulated basis.','What is the requirement shortfall?','CHF 2 million.','8 − 6 = 2 million. Positive capital still fails this hypothetical comparison.'),T('Another report gives accounting equity CHF 6 million and a different regulatory measure requiring 8 units.','No conversion or common measurement basis is supplied.','Can those two figures be compared directly?','No. The capital bases must first be made comparable.','A numeric subtraction does not establish compliance when the quantities are measured differently.')]),
  L('Technology and Digital Transformation','challenges',[19,21,32],
    'Technology and digital transformation concern changes to company processes, systems and information; the source names them as a challenge and an IT/digital function.',
    'Automation can change how information reaches underwriting, claims or reporting, but it does not decide what the contract means or guarantee better outcomes. The course names the challenge and the IT/digital strategy function, without prescribing a specific technology. A sound explanation asks which process changes, what information it uses and which controls still apply.',
    ['Identify the process and data that the technology changes.','Retain the supplied business rule or contract conditions when automating a decision.','Check outcomes and exception handling; a faster incorrect process is still incorrect.'],
    'The source gives no numerical efficiency gain, software recommendation or claim that all decisions should be automated. The examples specify an elementary decision rule solely to distinguish automation from its validity.',
    'Does automating a decision make its rule correct?','No. Automation executes the supplied rule. Its accuracy depends on whether the rule, inputs and exception handling are appropriate.',
    [T('An automated claims rule pays every reported loss immediately.','The contract excludes event X; a reported loss is event X.','Does speed make this rule consistent with the stated contract?','No. It pays a loss excluded by the supplied terms.','Automation does not replace the coverage check.'),T('The rule first rejects X and then pays a covered event Y.','Y is stipulated covered; all other payment conditions are satisfied.','What changed in the decision’s validity?','The rule now respects the stipulated coverage distinction for these inputs.','The meaningful improvement is applying the correct condition, not merely processing the same decision faster.')]),
  L('Climate Change and Emerging Risks','challenges',[21],
    'The challenges slide identifies catastrophe losses and emerging risks, including wildfires, floods, hurricanes, cyber and pandemic exposures.',
    'A new or changing hazard can alter how many claims occur together, their cost or which promises are exposed. The source’s examples are named risk categories, not quantified forecasts. A portfolio may have many policies and still concentrate exposure to one catastrophe. The question is which assumptions and shared drivers require reassessment.',
    ['Identify the named hazard and the relevant covered exposures.','Check whether a shared event can affect many contracts together.','Reassess stated claim, retention and capital assumptions without inventing frequencies or a mandatory response.'],
    'The slide does not supply a forecast, location-specific risk estimate or universal policy exclusion. Exercise losses and coverage are stipulated; they do not infer a climate trend or real cyber-policy entitlement.',
    'Why can a catastrophe challenge diversification even in a large portfolio?','One shared event can produce many correlated losses. The number of contracts does not establish independence from that common driver.',
    [T('One stipulated flood causes CHF 20,000 covered loss at each of 50 insured buildings.','All 50 are affected; ignore recoveries and expenses.','What is the aggregate loss?','CHF 1 million.','50 × 20,000 = 1,000,000. A shared hazard can affect many policies together.'),T('A portfolio adds a cyber exposure, but gives only the label “cyber”.','No probability, covered event or severity model is supplied.','Can its additional expected claim cost be calculated?','No. The exposure and cost assumptions are missing.','A newly named risk is not itself a quantitative forecast.')]),
  L('Response to claim frequency above plan','operations',[33,34,36],
    'A claim-frequency deviation can require coordinated claims, pricing, underwriting, actuarial and risk responses rather than a single automatic action.',
    'More claims than planned are information about an assumption, not an instruction to raise every premium immediately. Claims can investigate the observed experience, actuarial work compares it with the plan, pricing assesses cost consequences and underwriting considers the accepted risks. The source knowledge check chooses several functions together; the data supplied still determines which actions can be justified.',
    ['Measure the deviation on a comparable exposure and period basis.','Separate frequency from mean claim cost and investigate possible drivers.','Test implications with the relevant functions; do not promise that one response guarantees the target result.'],
    '“20% above plan” is a relative frequency increase, not necessarily a 20-percentage-point increase. The simplified cost model below holds exposure and severity fixed; no premium-adjustment rule is provided by the course.',
    'Does claim frequency 20% above plan automatically justify raising every premium by 20%?','No. First assess exposure comparability, severity, expenses, cover and causes; the course specifies coordinated functions rather than an automatic price rule.',
    [T('Planned claim frequency is 5%; it is reported as 20% above plan.','Relative increase; same exposure basis.','What is the resulting frequency?','6%, an increase of 1 percentage point.','5% × 1.20 = 6%. Adding 20 percentage points would answer a different question.'),T('Frequency is 20% higher, but mean claim cost is 20% lower.','Exposure is unchanged; expected claims cost is proportional to frequency × mean cost.','Must expected cost be 20% higher?','No. It becomes 96% of plan, a 4% decrease.','1.20 × 0.80 = 0.96. Frequency alone does not determine the combined cost effect.')]),
  L('Legal & General Counsel','functions',[32,33],
    'Legal and general counsel provides legal interpretation and advice for company decisions; the course names it separately from compliance.',
    'A product promise has legal wording and consequences that cannot be inferred from a pricing model. The organizational chart names Legal & General Counsel, and the value-chain table includes legal input in product design. Foundational role clarification: legal advice interprets rights, obligations and drafting questions; checking routine adherence to an already specified rule is a different task.',
    ['Identify the legal wording or obligation that the proposed decision depends on.','Obtain interpretation or drafting advice when that meaning is unresolved.','Coordinate with the business owner and compliance; advice is not an automatic guarantee that no dispute or violation can occur.'],
    'Source facts: Legal & General Counsel is a named function in a possible structure and legal work contributes to product design. The interpretation-versus-adherence distinction is declared foundational background. No jurisdiction’s law or particular contract interpretation is supplied.',
    'How does legal interpretation differ from checking compliance with a known rule?','Interpretation determines what an obligation or clause means; a compliance check compares conduct with a specified requirement. A chart naming both does not make those tasks interchangeable.',
    [T('A draft policy has two plausible meanings for its benefit clause.','Role convention: legal advises on meaning and wording; compliance checks adherence to an established requirement.','Which input is needed before checking conduct against this clause?','Resolve its meaning with legal interpretation or drafting advice.','An adherence check cannot uniquely apply a requirement whose meaning is still unresolved.'),T('The clause’s meaning is settled; a known approval step was skipped.','Same role convention; no interpretation ambiguity remains.','Is the immediate issue interpreting the clause again?','No. It is checking and addressing adherence to the known approval requirement.','The condition changed from uncertain meaning to a known rule and observed conduct.')]),
  L('IT/Digital Strategy (CTO)','functions',[21,32],
    'IT/digital strategy concerns systems and digital processes supporting company operations; it does not replace the business rule a system implements.',
    'A system can implement an underwriting or claims rule correctly and still need business review of the rule itself. The chart names IT/Digital strategy (CTO); the challenges slide names digital transformation. Foundational clarification separates technical execution, data handling and process integration from ownership of the insurance promise or acceptance criterion.',
    ['Identify the approved business rule, required data and system behavior.','Implement and test the process, including boundaries and failures.','Escalate a changed business criterion to its owner instead of assuming that a code change alone establishes its validity.'],
    'The chart’s CTO label is source-specific; it gives no universal reporting line, software choice or guaranteed efficiency gain. Functional responsibilities below are an explicitly declared teaching convention, not quoted job descriptions.',
    'Why must a system test distinguish rule implementation from business-rule validity?','A program can execute an inappropriate rule without errors. Technical tests establish execution against a specification, while business review determines whether that specification is appropriate.',
    [T('The approved rule accepts scores at most 5; software accepts score 7.','The criterion is correct for this exercise; IT is responsible for its implementation.','What kind of defect is demonstrated?','An implementation defect.','The system’s output contradicts the stipulated acceptance boundary.'),T('The software exactly applies the rule, but management proposes changing the boundary to 8.','The business owner must approve acceptance criteria.','Does editing the code settle whether 8 is appropriate?','No. The business criterion needs approval before implementation.','The uncertainty concerns the rule’s validity, not whether the program can execute it.')]),
  L('Investment Management (CIO)','functions',[31,32,33],
    'The source’s CIO investment function manages assets alongside liquidity, ALM and market/credit risk to support obligations, rather than maximizing return without constraints.',
    'The organizational title identifies a responsibility, while the balance-sheet slide explains the constraint: assets support future payments and have liquidity, market and credit risks. A proposed investment can offer a larger future amount yet fail a near-term payment need. The company function must consider those trade-offs with finance and risk, not promise that returns will fund every obligation.',
    ['Identify obligations and payment-date cash needs alongside the asset proposal.','Assess the proposal’s cash-flow timing and stated risks, not return alone.','Explain trade-offs and reporting consequences with the relevant functions.'],
    'Source scope: one possible structure labels Investment Mgmt (CIO). No optimal allocation or universal reporting line is taught. The exercise candidates are stipulated cash flows, not recommendations for real investments.',
    'Why is “choose the largest future return” incomplete for an insurer’s investment function?','The asset must also be assessed against payment timing, liquidity and market/credit risk. A future return cannot automatically fund an earlier obligation.',
    [T('Candidate A pays CHF 1.1 million in one year; B pays CHF 1 million tomorrow. A CHF 1 million claim is due tomorrow.','Neither asset can be sold or borrowed against; payments are stipulated certain.','Which candidate alone meets the stated payment-date need?','B.','B supplies the required cash by the due date; A’s larger later amount does not.'),T('The claim due date moves to one year, but candidate risks are unspecified.','Both candidates pay by the changed date; no risk or cost comparison is supplied.','Does timing alone now establish a complete investment preference?','No. It removes the prior timing exclusion but leaves other comparison inputs missing.','Resolving one constraint does not establish all market, credit, cost and return trade-offs.')]),
  L('Distribution & Partnerships','functions',[32,33],
    'Distribution and partnerships connect a defined insurance product with customers and delivery channels, alongside product, legal and compliance work.',
    'A promise must reach the intended customer through an operational channel, but reaching more customers does not by itself make the product suitable or profitable. The source names Distribution & Partnerships in the structure and distribution in the customer-need/product stage. Foundational clarification distinguishes delivery of the approved promise from changing its terms or accepting a particular risk.',
    ['Identify the intended need and approved product promise.','Coordinate the channel or partner that presents and delivers it.','Keep communicated terms aligned with the approved promise; route acceptance and legal questions to the relevant function.'],
    'The source supplies no universal broker arrangement, commission, suitability rule or guaranteed sales effect. The cases use an explicitly stipulated approved wording and partner communication.',
    'Does a distribution partner’s description automatically change the approved insurance benefit?','No. The communicated offer must be checked against the stipulated approved terms. Channel reach and the content of the contractual promise are separate issues.',
    [T('Approved wording pays on death during a 10-year term; a partner advertises payment on survival at year 10.','No survival benefit is approved in this exercise.','What discrepancy must the channel coordination address?','The partner describes a benefit absent from the approved promise.','Changing the communicated trigger is a substantive product mismatch, not merely a new sales channel.'),T('The partner accurately presents the term benefit, but asks to accept a risk that fails the stated underwriting rule.','Only underwriting may approve exceptions under this teaching convention.','Does correct presentation authorize acceptance?','No. The acceptance decision remains separate.','Communication accuracy resolves one issue; it does not override risk-acceptance criteria.')]),
  L('Compliance','functions',[32,33],
    'Compliance checks and supports adherence to applicable specified requirements; the source names it separately from legal advice and internal audit.',
    'A known requirement can be breached even when the product wording is clear. The chart names Compliance, and the product-stage table includes it with legal and distribution. Foundational role clarification: compare the proposed or observed process with applicable requirements, flag deviations and coordinate correction. Calling the function “Compliance” does not guarantee that all conduct is compliant.',
    ['Identify the applicable requirement and the evidence of what happened.','Compare the process with that requirement and identify any deviation.','Coordinate correction and documentation; seek legal interpretation if the requirement itself is unclear.'],
    'These duties are declared foundational background for the source’s named function. No actual law, approval authority or enforcement consequence is inferred from the organizational label.',
    'What is needed to establish a compliance deviation in a process?','A specified applicable requirement and evidence of the process. Without the rule or facts, the label alone cannot establish compliance or breach.',
    [T('A stipulated rule requires approval before release. A product is released before approval.','This rule applies and the timestamps are reliable.','Does the process meet the stated requirement?','No. Approval occurred too late for the required sequence.','The condition is approval before release, not approval at some eventual time.'),T('Approval was received before release, but its authority is unspecified.','Only a designated approver can satisfy the requirement.','Do the timestamps alone prove compliance?','No. The approver’s authority must also be established.','Satisfying the timing condition does not supply the missing authorization condition.')]),
  L('Risk Management/ERM','functions',[31,32,33],
    'Risk management/ERM examines risks across the company and their interaction with retained exposure and capital; it does not eliminate uncertainty.',
    'Different teams may see only their own part of a shared event. A catastrophe can increase claims and also impair assets, so adding isolated departmental assessments may miss their interaction. The source names Risk Mgmt/ERM and risk/capital management. The enterprise-wide perspective is foundational clarification, not a guarantee of resilience or a source-specified capital model.',
    ['Identify relevant claim, asset and operational exposures under the stated scenario.','Consider shared drivers and effects together rather than assuming independence.','Communicate retained exposure, limits and trade-offs with actuarial, investment and capital functions.'],
    'The source supplies no universal ERM procedure, risk appetite threshold or probability model. Exercise scenario costs are stipulated and additive; no diversification benefit is assumed.',
    'Why can separate departmental risk figures miss a common shock?','The same event can affect several exposures together. An enterprise view examines that interaction rather than treating the figures as unrelated or mutually exclusive.',
    [T('One scenario adds CHF 2 million claim costs and CHF 1 million asset losses.','Both occur together; capital starts at CHF 4 million and absorbs both effects.','What buffer remains in this simplified scenario?','CHF 1 million.','4 − 2 − 1 = 1 million. Assessing only the claim side would miss the asset effect.'),T('Two departments each describe “the same CHF 2 million claim loss”.','They refer to the identical loss, not separate losses.','Should the enterprise total be CHF 4 million?','No. Count the identical loss once.','Integration requires checking identity as well as interaction; adding duplicated reports overstates exposure.')]),
  L('Finance & Accounting (CFO)','functions',[31,32,33],
    'Finance and accounting record and report financial effects, connecting assets, obligations, equity and period result on the specified accounting basis.',
    'A payment, a liability-estimate revision and a profit figure tell different financial stories. The chart names Finance & Accounting (CFO), and the balance-sheet and value-chain slides connect finance/reporting with profitability, solvency and disclosures. Foundational clarification supplies a simplified accounting model so the learner can trace an event without confusing cash with the result.',
    ['Identify whether the event changes cash, an estimated obligation, a period expense or several together.','Record the specified effects consistently on the assumed accounting basis.','Report the result and financial position without treating accounting equity as a complete regulatory solvency measure.'],
    'Illustrative convention: equity = assets − liabilities; an expense-driven provision increase reduces equity while no cash moves; settlement at the existing estimate reduces cash and liability equally. Ignore tax and other adjustments. These are declared basic accounting explanations, not complete source-taught standards.',
    'Why can a claim expense reduce the result before any cash payment occurs?','Recognizing an increased estimated obligation can create an expense on the stipulated accounting basis. Cash moves only on payment; expense recognition and settlement are separate events.',
    [T('A claim provision increases CHF 100; no payment occurs.','Increase is an expense; no tax or other change.','What happens to cash and equity?','Cash is unchanged; equity falls CHF 100.','The liability increases while assets stay fixed under equity = assets − liabilities.'),T('An already recognized CHF 100 liability is paid at its estimate.','Cash and the liability each fall CHF 100; no new expense.','Does this settlement reduce equity again?','No. Equity is unchanged by this settlement.','(Assets − 100) − (Liabilities − 100) = Assets − Liabilities. The expense was recognized earlier.')]),
  L('HR/People Management','functions',[32],
    'The possible company structure names HR/People Management as a people-related function, distinct from accepting insurance risks.',
    'An insurer needs employees and capabilities to carry out pricing, claims and other work. The chart names HR/People Mgmt but does not give a detailed job description. Foundational clarification concerns staffing and employee development: choosing or training staff is a different problem from underwriting a customer’s insurance exposure.',
    ['Identify whether the decision concerns employees or insured customer risks.','Use the stipulated staffing or skills requirement for the people decision.','Coordinate with the relevant team without substituting HR approval for risk-acceptance authority.'],
    'Only the function’s name and presence are source facts. Staffing and development are declared general background; no labor-law rule, hiring criterion or reporting line is inferred from the chart.',
    'Why is selecting an employee different from underwriting an insurance customer?','The first concerns staffing and capabilities; the second accepts an insured exposure on stated terms. A similar word such as “selection” does not make them one function.',
    [T('The claims team needs an employee trained in its approved workflow.','Teaching convention: HR coordinates staffing/training; underwriting owns customer-risk acceptance.','Which responsibility is involved?','People management and team training.','The decision concerns an employee capability, not a policyholder’s risk.'),T('A customer’s proposed policy fails an acceptance criterion.','Same responsibility convention; no staffing issue.','Can HR approval replace the required underwriting decision?','No. This is a customer-risk acceptance issue.','Changing the subject of the decision changes the responsible function.')]),
  L('Audit (internal control)','functions',[32,33],
    'The source names Audit (internal control) and includes audit in investment/reporting; checking controls differs from performing each business decision.',
    'A control can exist on paper without operating as intended. The organizational chart names the audit/internal-control function. Foundational clarification asks what evidence demonstrates that a specified control actually operated. That review is different from pricing a risk or issuing the operational approval itself; one successful check does not guarantee that every process is sound.',
    ['Identify the specified control and what its operation should leave as evidence.','Check the supplied records against that control and report gaps.','Distinguish evidence from assumptions and a limited sample from a universal conclusion.'],
    'The chart does not specify an auditing standard, universal independence structure or assurance level. The examples use a stated control and sample only; they are not conclusions about a real company audit.',
    'Does a documented control prove that it operated for every transaction?','No. Its design and its operation are different questions. Records and a defined review scope are needed to assess whether it actually operated.',
    [T('A control requires an approval record for every release; one sampled release has no record.','The sample and missing record are established.','What does this evidence justify reporting?','A missing approval record for that sampled release.','It demonstrates a gap in that case, not the status of every other release.'),T('All 10 sampled releases have the required record.','The company has 1,000 releases; no other test evidence is supplied.','Is operation for all 1,000 releases proved?','No. The supplied evidence concerns the 10 sampled cases.','A limited sample cannot be described as direct verification of every transaction.')]),
];

